 "post": {
  "title": "One Bad Day Ruined the Whole Trip #shorts",
  "description": "Six perfect days of holiday, one cancelled flight, and Arthur calls the whole trip a disaster. Psychologists call it the peak-end rule: we judge an experience by its most intense moment and its ending, not by how long it lasted. In a 1993 study (Kahneman, Fredrickson, Schreiber & Redelmeier, \"When more pain is preferred to less\"), people held a hand in 14°C water for 60 seconds, and in another trial for 90 seconds where the last 30 were slightly warmer. Nearly 7 in 10 chose to repeat the longer one. So end your dates, workouts and meetings on a high. #psychology #shorts #memory #peakendrule",
